,"h":0.01356,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"0 L"},{"x":0.3626,"y":0.25872,"w":0.58892,"h":0.03858,"fs":0.0131,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"we run into a problem because 1((sinx)>x) dx cannot be expressed in terms of elemen- tary functions (there is no simple antiderivative)."},{"x":0.3626,"y":0.29985,"w":0.59308,"h":0.04348,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"There is no general rule for predicting which order of integration will be the good one in circumstances like these. If the order you first choose doesn’t work, try the other. Some- times neither order will work, and then we need to use numerical approximations."},{"x":0.10184,"y":0.31209,"w":0.11176,"h":0.03021,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"R 1 x2 + y2 = 1"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.36727,"w":0.21933,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Finding Limits of Integration"},{"x":0.11796,"y":0.37347,"w":0.05306,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"x + y = 1"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.39193,"w":0.59297,"h":0.04348,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"We now give a procedure for finding limits of integration that applies for many regions in the plane. Regions that are more complicated, and for which this procedure fails, can often be split up into pieces on which the procedure works."},{"x":0.10184,"y":0.39776,"w":0.10705,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"0 1"},{"x":0.16007,"y":0.42296,"w":0.01453,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(a)"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.44479,"w":0.59303,"h":0.02899,"fs":0.0131,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Using Vertical Cross-Sections When faced with evaluating 4R ƒ(x, y) dA, integrating first with respect to y and then with respect to x, do the following three steps:"},{"x":0.18327,"y":0.45871,"w":0.07246,"h":0.02379,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"Leaves at y = \"1 −x2"},{"x":0.10184,"y":0.47732,"w":0.04019,"h":0.02065,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"1 R"},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.48399,"w":0.59303,"h":0.09722,"fs":0.01279,"n":6,"k":1,"t":"1. Sketch. Sketch the region of integration and label the bounding curves (Figure 15.14a). 2